s four `tmp()` calls and the same logic written twice.\n   - Add `function sandbox() { const d = tmp('chrome-'); return { flag: \\`--user-data-dir=${d}/profile\\`, env: { ...process.env, TMPDIR: d } } }`. Chromium creates the profile dir itself, and its `org.chromium.*` scratch dirs land beside it under the same root.\n   - Both call sites then shrink to `sandbox()`, and there is one dir per launch instead of two. This is about 10 lines.\n\n2. **`test/tmp.test.ts` lines 22-28 (group `guard`): put the expected exit code in the `scripts` table.**\n   - The nested ternary `name === 'exits normally' ? 0 : name === 'throws' ? 1 : 3` repeats the keys of the table above it.\n   - Make the table `{ tail, status }` and use `status` directly. This is a 5-line change, and a new case can't leave the two out of sync.\n\n3. **`test/tmp.test.ts` lines 20-24 (group `guard`): run the child with `node --input-type=module -e <code>` instead of a script file.**\n   - The script is currently written to `join(fresh, '..', …)`, which is the shared `/tmp` itself. That is the place this change is trying to keep clean, and a crashed run would leave a `.mts` file there.\n   - Passing the code with `-e` removes the file write, the `finally`/`rmSync` and the `name.replace(/\\W/g, '')` filename munging. It is about 4 lines fewer.\n   - It depends on the Node version supporting `.ts` imports from eval. The tests already import `.ts` files, so it likely does.\n\n4. **`test/tmp.ts`: consider dropping the `onCleanup` registry (optional).**\n   - `onCleanup` and `cleanups` exist only so `browse` can SIGKILL Chromium before its profile is removed.\n   - The same effect comes from `process.on('exit', () => chrome.kill('SIGKILL'))` inside `browse`, plus `process.off` in the `finally`. That removes a Set, an exported function and a try/catch loop from the helper.\n   - Handlers on `exit` run in registration order, and `browse`'s runs before the root's only if the root was created first. That is true here