ium puts its `SingletonSocket` under `TMPDIR`, and Unix socket paths are limited to about 108 bytes. On this machine the path is over the limit. Chromium aborts with `FATAL: Socket path too long`, and `browse()` never gets a `/json` target, so it throws at `browser.ts:79`.\n   - Base commit (HEAD~1) `test/reviews.test.ts`: 15/15 pass.\n   - Working tree, `TMPDIR` set to a scratch dir: 12 pass, 3 fail. Reproduced in isolation: `--test-name-pattern=\"a #reviews-\"` fails on the working tree and passes on base.\n   - Working tree with the default `TMPDIR` (`/tmp`): 15/15 pass.\n   - The full `npm test` run also fails in many browser tests (about 20 failures) under the scratch `TMPDIR`.\n   - The author's \"fresh-TMPDIR run leaves 0 entries\" evidence is consistent with this. The suite does not pass under a long per-job `TMPDIR`, which is the setup the TODO note asks reviewers to use.\n2. **`use-tmp` (mechanical), read:** the summary says \"~45 unremoved mkdtemp dirs\". The diff replaces 37 `mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), …))` calls in test files. The \"45\" figure is unsupported.\n3. **`todo` (supporting), read:** the note tells reviewers to set `TMPDIR` to a per-job dir. That is the exact setting that triggers the socket failure above, so the note would send people into the bug.\n\n**Misgraded necessity**\n\n4. **`chromium` is labelled required, but only part of it is.** The required part is the `onCleanup` kill and the `exited` wait. The per-launch dir and the `TMPDIR` override are not needed to stop the leak, since the process root already gets removed on exit. The root alone would stop the leak. The `TMPDIR` override is the part that causes the regression, and its only benefit is isolation between launches.\n5. **`guard` (supporting), verified: accurate.** With the `exit` handler disabled, 3/3 guard tests fail. With it restored, 3/3 pass. The guard test also uses its own `mkdtempSync(tmpdir())`, which is fine.\n\n**Removal and simplification candidates**\n\n6. **Drop the `TMPDIR` override in `sandbox()`** and keep only the per-launch `--user-data-dir`. This removes the regression. Chromium's `org.chromium.*` scratch then goes back to `/tmp` as it did on base, so the leak it was meant to fix would return. If that matters, the alternative is a short root name (for example `gg-` and `c-` prefixes) and no per-job `TMPDIR` in the docs. Either way the reproduction is `reviews.test.ts` under a long `TMPDIR`.\n7. **`todo` (h1)** can go or be reworded until the socket issue is fixed. The note as written is wrong for this setup.\n\n**Missing risks**\n\n8. **`dumpDom` (h6), read:** unlike `browse`, it has no `onCleanup` kill. If `execFileSync` times out and kills only the parent process, Chromium children can keep running and write into a root that gets removed on exit.\n9. **Socket path length (verified above):** the new layout puts a socket path near 108 bytes whenever `TMPDIR` is long. Any test that passes an overlong path will fail the same way, so this needs a test or a guard, not only docs.\n10. **SIGKILL (listed by the author):** the root is not removed. The `guard` tests cannot cover this. Keep it in the risk list.\n11. **Node engine range (`>=20.12`), read:** the guard test runs `process.execPath` on a `.ts` file with no strip-types flags. This works on Node 26 (what I ran). I did not check it on Node 20 or 22, where that may fail.\n\nVerified checks: `tsc` exits 0. The base and working-tree runs confirm the leak is gone (the base leaves about 40 `reviews-*` dirs and six `org.chromium.*` dirs in `TMPDIR`; the working tree leaves none).\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
