ted by then, `forget()` still unregisters the SIGKILL hook.\n   - **Outcome:** Chromium may still be running and writing into its profile when the exit handler runs `rmSync` on the root. That can leave profile directories behind, or have Chromium recreate files after they are removed. The hook should only be forgotten once Chromium has actually exited.\n   - **Related:** `kill()` signals only the main process, so helper processes can outlive it. They keep the profile open the same way.\n\n2. **minor, read: the cleanup comment overstates when it runs.** `test/tmp.ts`, lines 5–6 and 20–24.\n   - **Scenario:** The cleanup is registered with `process.on('exit')`. It does not run if the test process is killed by SIGTERM or SIGINT, for example when the test runner times out or the user presses Ctrl-C. SIGKILL and an OOM kill also skip it.\n   - **Outcome:** The comment says the root is removed \"also after a failing test or a crash\" and \"however it exits\", and `tmp.test.ts` says the same. Only normal exit, an uncaught throw and `process.exit` are covered. After an interrupted run, `gloss-run-*` directories and any live Chromium profiles stay in /tmp, which is the leak this change is meant to stop.\n   - **Fix:** Add SIGINT and SIGTERM handlers that call `process.exit`.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
